# Onboarding Policy
**Daisy Health**
**Policy ID:** HR-OB-005
**Effective Date:** January 1, 2025
**Last Reviewed:** June 1, 2025
**Owner:** People Operations

---

## Purpose

A strong start sets employees up for long-term success. This policy describes what new Daisy Health employees can expect during their first 90 days, what is required of them, and how the onboarding experience differs for clinical and non-clinical staff.

---

## Scope

This policy applies to all new full-time and part-time Daisy Health employees. Contractors follow a separate onboarding process managed by their engagement lead.

---

## 1. Before Your First Day

People Operations will send a welcome email within 48 hours of your accepted offer. This email includes:

- A link to complete your new hire paperwork (I-9, W-4, direct deposit, emergency contacts)
- Instructions for setting up your Daisy Health email and accounts
- Your start date, first-day schedule, and manager contact information
- A link to the Benefits Enrollment Portal — you have 30 days from your start date to enroll

Your company laptop will be shipped to your home address and should arrive by your first day. If it does not arrive on time, notify your manager and IT at it@daisyhealth.com immediately.

---

## 2. First Day

On your first day, you will:

- Attend a virtual New Hire Orientation session (9:00 AM – 12:00 PM local time)
- Meet your onboarding buddy — a peer assigned to help you get settled
- Complete account setup with IT support (email, VPN, Slack, EHR access for clinical staff)
- Review and sign your confidentiality and data security agreements
- Begin required compliance training (see Section 4)

Your manager will schedule a 1:1 welcome meeting on your first day to review your first-week priorities and answer questions.

---

## 3. First 30 Days

During your first 30 days, you are expected to:

- Complete all required onboarding training (see Section 4)
- Enroll in benefits through the Benefits Enrollment Portal
- Set up your home office workspace
- Meet with your key cross-functional partners and stakeholders
- Understand your team's workflows, tools, and communication norms
- Review Daisy Health's mission, values, and strategic priorities

Your manager will hold a **30-day check-in meeting** to discuss early impressions, clarify role expectations, and address any questions.

---

## 4. Required Onboarding Training

All new employees must complete the following training through the Daisy Health Learning Management System (LMS) within their first 30 days:

| Training Module | Audience | Due |
|---|---|---|
| HIPAA Fundamentals | All staff | Day 7 |
| Data Security and Device Policy | All staff | Day 7 |
| Workplace Conduct and Code of Ethics | All staff | Day 14 |
| Benefits Overview and Enrollment | All staff | Day 14 |
| Telehealth Platform Training | Clinical staff | Day 7 |
| EHR Documentation Standards | Clinical staff | Day 14 |
| Licensure and Credentialing Overview | Clinical staff | Day 14 |
| Prescribing and Scope of Practice | Physicians, NPs, PAs | Day 21 |

Training completion is tracked by People Operations. Employees who have not completed required training by the deadlines above will receive a reminder from their manager. Persistent non-completion may delay full system access.

---

## 5. Days 31–60

During your second month, you will:

- Take on initial independent responsibilities in your role
- Begin participating in team and department meetings as an active contributor
- Continue learning Daisy Health's product, patient population, and care model
- Complete any role-specific training assigned by your manager
- Have a **60-day check-in** with your manager to assess progress against role expectations

Clinical staff will begin supervised patient care during this period, with supervision frequency determined by role, licensure, and state requirements.

---

## 6. Days 61–90

By the end of 90 days, employees are expected to:

- Be fully independent in core job responsibilities
- Have completed all required onboarding training
- Understand their performance goals and how success is measured
- Feel comfortable navigating Daisy Health's tools, systems, and culture

Your manager will conduct a **formal 90-day review** to discuss performance, answer questions, and confirm your continued fit in the role. This is a two-way conversation — you are encouraged to share feedback about your onboarding experience.

---

## 7. Clinical Staff Onboarding — Additional Steps

Clinical employees (physicians, NPs, PAs, RNs, clinical pharmacists, behavioral health counselors) have additional onboarding requirements:

### Background Check and Licensure Verification

All clinical staff must pass a comprehensive background check and have their professional licenses verified by the Credentialing team before seeing patients. This process begins upon offer acceptance and typically takes 5–10 business days.

Do not see patients until you receive written confirmation from the Credentialing team that your verification is complete.

### Credentialing with Health Plans

For clinical staff who will bill insurance, credentialing with Daisy Health's health plan partners is required. This process can take **60–120 days** and is initiated by the Credentialing team upon hire. You will receive updates via email as your credentialing progresses.

### Supervised Clinical Period

During your first 30 days of patient care, a senior clinician will review a sample of your patient encounters and provide feedback. The volume and duration of supervision varies by role:

- Physicians: 2 weeks of structured peer review
- NPs and PAs: 4 weeks, per state collaborative practice requirements
- Clinical pharmacists: 2 weeks with clinical pharmacy lead
- Behavioral health counselors: 4 weeks with licensed supervisor

---

## 8. Onboarding Buddy Program

Every new employee is paired with an **onboarding buddy** — a peer who has been at Daisy Health for at least 6 months. Your buddy is not your manager. They are a friendly resource to help you understand the culture, navigate tools, and get comfortable quickly.

Buddies are expected to:

- Check in with you at least once per week during your first 30 days
- Answer informal questions about how things work at Daisy Health
- Introduce you to other team members
- Share their own onboarding experience and tips

---

## 9. New Hire Probationary Period

All new employees are subject to a **90-day introductory period**. During this time:

- Employment may be ended by either party with no notice requirement (at-will employment)
- PTO accrues but may not be taken during the first 30 days without manager approval
- Employees are not eligible for internal transfers during the probationary period

Successful completion of the 90-day period does not change your at-will employment status.

